a federal jury convicted elizabeth  holmes on four of 11 charges for her  role in a years-long fraud scheme while  running her startup theranos over the  course of the high-profile trial the  prosecution set out to prove that holmes  intentionally misled investors and  patients about the efficacy of  theranos's blood testing technology  jurors heard from 29 government  witnesses and reviewed reams of  documents text messages emails and  reports  they also heard directly from holmes  herself who spent seven days on the  stand she acknowledged regrets but  maintained that she never defrauded  anyone the jury found her not guilty on  four other counts and failed to reach a  verdict on three counts with cameras  banned in the courtroom wsj's sarah  randazzo brings us all the highlights  from holmes's testimony  elizabeth holmes shocked a lot of people  when she took the stand what was the  defense's strategy in having her talk  she's good at working a room she's good  at convincing people it's what she had  done  her whole career she had convinced tons  of high profile investors and washington  luminaries and the like to buy into her  vision and really this was just the  ultimate sales pitch for taking the  stand to tell her side of the story in  the first part of her testimony the  defense walked the jury through the  early days of theranos  they painted her as an entrepreneur who  had great intentions and was really  trying to improve healthcare she said  she thought things were going well and  you know she'd get reports from a study  saying oh these results were excellent  and she wanted to share that with  investors and so she gave a lot of  examples of times when scientists and  others were telling her things were on  track  another big topic throughout the trial  holmes comments about her company's  relationship with the military here's a  clip prosecutors played of holmes  talking to investors in 2013. we built  the business around  our partnerships with pharmaceutical  companies and our contracts with the  military  wherein we could deploy  our framework a lot of investors said  that they backed theranos because of its  relationship with the military which  didn't exist  how did holmes answer to that she  basically insisted uh you know up down  and sideways that she did not recall  ever telling an investor that the  fairness device is being actively used  by the military and so it was this very  odd disconnect where we had heard from  several investors who said she said this  in pitches and that was a big thing that  we  you know that got us to invest holmes  explained that theranos was jointly  working on several studies with the us  department of defense but she  acknowledged that no military  partnerships ever resulted from the  studies another key moment came when  holmes conceded to one of the  key pieces of evidence she admitted to  altering theranos documents to include  the logos of major pharmaceutical  companies  investors testified that the doctored  reports helped convince them to buy into  the startup  and she said yes i i added the logos of  pharmaceutical companies to the top but  i didn't think that was misleading i  just thought it showed that this report  was done in consultation with the  pharmaceutical companies so she didn't  frame it in the same way the prosecution  did even as she said she was the one  responsible for it another key issue in  the trial was theranose's use of blood  testing devices made by other companies  prosecutors played this video clip of  holmes speaking with cnbc's jim cramer  in 2015. every test that we offer in our  laboratory can run on our proprietary  devices holmes falsely claimed theranos  could run more than 200 tests on its  proprietary device in reality the device  could only perform a dozen tests we  heard her basically say publicly for the  first time what they did with these  third-party devices and that yes they  bought third-party machines and modified  them so that they could run their tiny  blood samples that you know they said  were being run on their own proprietary  machines a pivotal moment in holmes's  testimony came when she claimed she had  suffered sexual and emotional abuse it  was of course very traumatic and and  serious allegations that she brought  saying that her former boyfriend sonny  belwani who was also her top deputy  theranos really controlled her life on a  micro level controlling when she could  sleep when she could eat  how she ran her business what she should  do to be a better business person and  even saying that he forced her to have  sex when she didn't want to sonny  balwani has denied the abuse allegations  prosecutors presented evidence in the  form of hundreds of text messages  between holmes and bahawani showing  loving exchanges  bahwani faces a separate trial next year  for the same fraud and conspiracy  charges as holmes he has denied the  allegations against him it really was  emotional powerful testimony  the problem was it didn't relate to the  fraud that was being alleged that it  happened she even explicitly said no i'm  not saying that sunny told me what to  tell investors and what to tell business  partners but she was saying he clouded  kind of everything about what she did in  the prosecution's cross-examination  holmes expressed remorse for how  theranos treated lab employees and  handled situations with reporters  prosecutors told jurors how theranos  threatened scientists with legal action  to keep them from talking to journalists  about problems at the lab holmes also  testified about her attempts to quash  wall street journal reporting the  journal broke story after story about  theranose's failed technology i think  the strategy there is to make her look  more human if you take the stand and say  i did nothing wrong i've never done  anything wrong in my life but a jury's  going to have a hard time relating to  that when she said things like the way  we handled the wall street journal  process was a complete disaster and yes  i really wish we had treated erica chan  one of the lab scientists better you  know we could have done that differently  it showed she was taking some  responsibility  holmes's final verdict could have a  lasting impact in the world of silicon  valley venture capital startups white  collar cases are very hard to bring  fraud is very hard to prove and you know  there's always a lot of questions over  whether it's worth it for the government  to bring a case like this and so this  could give other companies in silicon  valley pause but it certainly is notable  in that it's very rare um for startup  and startup culture to be considered a  crime what does this decision mean for  homes sentencing will follow we don't  know yet um you know what kind of  sentence she'll have but each wire fraud  count carries the maximum sentence of 20  years and so  she's looking at potentially years in  prison a lot of factors go into  sentencing it's really a complex matrix  where they take into account prior  convictions things like that which  obviously she doesn't have so  it'll be really interesting to see what  the judge does with sentencing
 

